14:12:53.234502 IP (tos 0xc0, ttl 255, id 120, offset 0, flags [DF], proto TCP (6), length 60)The cksum 0x0e31 (incorrect -> ...) message is not a real network error or a packet issue. It is a side effect of TCP Checksum Offloading.Your SONiC switch's network interface card (NIC) or hypervisor handles TCP checksum calculations in hardware right before sending the packet onto the physical wire. Because tcpdump intercepts outbound packets while they are still in the OS kernel—before they reach the hardware—the checksum field is blank or filled with filler data, causing tcpdump to report it as incorrect.The packets actually arrive at the neighboring device with perfectly valid checksums.How to Hide the "Incorrect" MessagesYou can use the -K flag to tell tcpdump to ignore checksum verification. This cleans up your output so you can focus on debugging your BGP session (TCP port 179).Modify your command string to include -K (or --dont-verify-checksums):bashssh Border_Leaf1 "sudo timeout 15 tcpdump -K -i Ethernet0 -n -v 'tcp port 179' 2>&1 | head -20"
Use code with caution.Actual BGP Issue Noted in the OutputWhile the checksum error is harmless, your packet capture reveals a real networking issue preventing your BGP session from establishing:Asymmetric MSS/MTU: 10.0.253.1 is sending SYN packets with an MSS of 1460 (Standard 1500 MTU). However, 10.0.253.0 is replying with a SYN packet featuring an MSS of 9060 (Jumbo Frames).Missing ACKs: You can see 10.0.253.1 sending a SYN ([S]), 10.0.253.0 replying with a SYN-ACK ([S.]), but the final [.] ACK is never sent. The handshake is failing, and the routers keep retrying.If you want to troubleshoot why the BGP connection is failing to complete the 3-way handshake, let me know if these are virtual devices (like community SONiC in GNS3/EVE-NG) or physical switches, and we can check the MTU configuration.You said: What needs to happen to receive SYN-ACK in this virtual environmentTo receive and successfully process the SYN-ACK packet in this virtual environment, you do not need to generate a new packet—the SYN-ACK is already being received by 10.0.253.1.The real breakdown is that 10.0.253.1 is dropping the SYN-ACK internally instead of replying with the final ACK to finish the 3-way handshake. This happens because the virtual interfaces have mismatched Maximum Transmission Unit (MTU) / Maximum Segment Size (MSS) settings, causing the Linux kernel or the virtual switch to drop the frame as invalid.To fix the drop and complete the connection, execute the following two steps.1. Match the MTU on Both SwitchesYour tcpdump output shows 10.0.253.0 advertising an MSS of 9060 (implying a Jumbo Frame MTU of 9080+), while 10.0.253.1 is advertising 1460 (Standard 1500 MTU). In virtualized networking environments (like GNS3, EVE-NG, or KVM), mismatched MTUs across a virtual link frequently cause the hypervisor's virtual bridge to silently drop packets.Run these commands inside the SONiC CLI on both switches to standardize them to standard frames (1500 MTU):bash# Log into both switches and set the interface MTU to 1500
sudo config interface mtu Ethernet0 1500
Use code with caution.Alternatively, if you want Jumbo Frames, set both sides to 9100.2. Configure IP MTU on the Interface Vlan / L3 InterfaceIf Ethernet0 belongs to a VLAN (e.g., Vlan100) or is routed via a PortChannel, the Layer 3 MTU must also match.Check your current configuration profiles:bashshow interface status Ethernet0
show ip interfaces
Use code with caution.If it is bound to a VLAN interface, match the MTU there as well:bashsudo config vlan mtu <vlan_id> 1500
Use code with caution.Why This Fixes ItOnce both virtual switches agree on the MTU, the TCP handshake negotiation will look like this:10.0.253.1 sends a SYN with mss 1460.10.0.253.0 receives it and responds with a SYN-ACK bound to mss 1460 (clamping down to the lowest common denominator).The Linux kernel on 10.0.253.1 accepts the packet rather than discarding it, and immediately sends the final ACK to move the BGP state from Connect/Active to Established.If you change the MTU settings and the handshake still fails, run show ip bgp neighbors on both sides so we can check if a BGP peer-group or TTL security setting is causing an administrative block.
